// GENERATED by scripts/bundle-site-craft.mjs from the site-craft skill — do not edit by hand.
export const SITE_CRAFT_SKILL: string = "<file path=\"SKILL.md\">\n---\nname: site-craft\ndescription: Design and build award-level websites of any type (e-commerce store, business/service, SaaS/landing, portfolio/blog) with research-backed UI/UX — layout, alignment, typography, color, 3D elements, motion, conversion and accessibility — then self-check with screenshots and a QA checklist. Use when asked to design, build, redesign or polish a website, landing page, storefront or web UI, or when a site \"looks generic / AI-made\" and needs to look premium.\n---\n\n# Site Craft\n\nBuild websites that look designed by a top studio and convert like they were tested by a growth team. Every rule here comes from published research (NN/g, Baymard Institute, WCAG 2.2, Google web.dev, Apple HIG, Material Design) or from shipped, award-level sites. Sources are listed in `references/sources.md`.\n\n**The standard:** a visitor should understand *what this is, who it's for and what to do next* in 5 seconds, and the site should feel intentional in every pixel — nothing default, nothing random.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip)\n\n### 1. Discover (ask before designing)\n\nAsk the client these, in one short message. If they don't know, pick a sensible default and say so.\n\n1. **Goal:** what one action counts as success? (buy, call, book, sign up, hire, read)\n2. **Audience:** who, what device mostly, what they fear or want, local or global?\n3. **Brand:** name, logo, existing colors/fonts, 3 words for how it should *feel*.\n4. **References:** 2–3 sites they like (and why), competitors.\n5. **Content:** what they actually have — photos, products, prices, testimonials, numbers, copy.\n6. **Constraints:** stack/CMS, languages (RTL?), deadline, budget for 3D/video.\n\nNever invent facts about the business (prices, stats, client names, testimonials). Use clearly marked placeholders.\n\n### 2. Choose direction\n\n1. Identify the site type and read its guide:\n   - Store → `references/site-types/ecommerce.md`\n   - Business / service / local / agency → `references/site-types/business.md`\n   - SaaS / product / app landing → `references/site-types/saas.md`\n   - Portfolio / personal / blog → `references/site-types/portfolio.md`\n2. **Art direction first (mandatory, the step that prevents \"AI slop\"):** read `references/art-direction.md`, look at 5–10 award-level references, and write the art direction brief: one-line concept, one signature moment, image direction, type with a point of view, palette from the concept, an unusual layout idea, voice, and 3 template moves you will NOT make. Show the client the concept line and signature moment before building.\n3. Pick the closest style from `references/styles.md` as a starting point only — the concept overrides it. Offer the client 2 options in one line each; recommend one.\n4. **Imagery plan:** list every image the page needs and where it comes from (client photos, AI-generated in one art direction, licensed photos). Never draw products/people with SVG or CSS shapes; use labelled placeholders if nothing is available yet.\n5. Write the **design tokens** before any section (template in `references/foundations.md` → \"Token sheet\"): colors, fonts, type scale, spacing, radius, shadow, easing, durations. All components use tokens only — no magic numbers.\n\n### 3. Build\n\n1. Lay out the page skeleton from the site-type guide's section order. Content hierarchy first, decoration last.\n2. Build mobile-first, then enhance at 768px and 1024px+.\n3. Apply, in this order:\n   - Layout, grid and alignment → `references/foundations.md`\n   - Typography → `references/foundations.md`\n   - Color → `references/color.md`\n   - Components and states → `references/components.md`\n   - Motion → `references/motion.md`\n   - 3D (only if it serves the goal) → `references/3d.md`\n   - **Experience layer → `references/studio-grade.md`** (entrance gate, full-bleed wordmark, poster-composed hero, living object, overlapping sections, sticky stacking cards, pill nav / bottom tab bar, smooth scroll, giant headings, micro-life). Use at least 5 of 10 on brand/marketing home pages — this is what separates \"good landing page\" from award-level.\n4. Every section must answer: *what is this section's one job?* If it has two jobs, split it. If it has none, delete it.\n\n### 4. Self-check (mandatory)\n\n1. Screenshot at **375 × 812**, **768 × 1024** and **1440 × 900** (and a full-page shot). If you have a browser tool with device emulation, use it; otherwise Playwright with a device preset. Plain `chrome --headless --window-size=375,...` does NOT give a real 375px viewport (Chrome enforces a ~500px minimum window), so mobile shots from it are wrong. Very tall full-page windows also stretch `100vh/svh` heroes; cap hero height (e.g. `min(100svh, 960px)`). Bust the cache (`?v=N`) after edits.\n2. Look at each screenshot and run `references/qa-checklist.md`. Fix every failure, re-screenshot, repeat.\n3. **Taste critique** (separate from QA — a page can pass every QA item and still be slop):\n   - Run the \"2026 AI-default look\" list in `references/anti-patterns.md`. 3+ hits = redesign the hero and section shapes, don't patch.\n   - Logo-swap test: replace the brand name with a competitor's. If the page still works, the concept isn't on the page.\n   - Template test: could this layout come from a free theme? If yes, change at least the hero and two section shapes.\n   - Describe-it test: in one sentence, what would a visitor tell a friend about this site? If the answer is \"it looked nice\", there is no signature moment.\n   - Compare the screenshots side by side with your references. Be honest about which looks more designed.\n4. Report to the client: what was built, which style and why, what is placeholder, what is not verified.\n\n## Non-negotiables\n\n- **Correct is not enough.** Every rule below makes a site correct; only the concept, signature moment and imagery make it memorable. Never skip art direction.\n- **No drawn products or people.** Real or generated photography, 3D renders, or clearly labelled placeholders.\n\n- **5-second test:** the hero states what it is + for whom + one primary CTA, above the fold on mobile.\n- **One primary action per screen.** Secondary actions look secondary.\n- **Alignment:** every element snaps to the grid and to a shared edge. No \"almost aligned\".\n- **Spacing:** 8-point scale only (4 for tight UI). Space between groups > space within groups.\n- **Type:** max 2 families (+ optional mono). Body ≥ 16px, line length 45–75 characters.\n- **Contrast:** text ≥ 4.5:1 (large text ≥ 3:1), UI parts and focus rings ≥ 3:1 (WCAG 2.2).\n- **Targets:** tap targets ≥ 44 × 44 px with ≥ 8px between them.\n- **Speed:** LCP ≤ 2.5s, INP ≤ 200ms, CLS ≤ 0.1. Hero image/3D never blocks first paint.\n- **Motion respects `prefers-reduced-motion`.** 3D always has a static fallback.\n- **Real states:** hover, focus-visible, active, disabled, loading, empty, error, success for every interactive thing.\n- **Semantic HTML first:** landmarks, one `h1`, ordered headings, labels on inputs, alt text.\n\n## Reference map\n\n| File | Use it for |\n|---|---|\n| `references/art-direction.md` | Concept, signature moment, imagery, type/color with a point of view, breaking template rhythm — read FIRST |\n| `references/studio-grade.md` | The experience layer: entrance, wordmark, poster hero, living object, stacking cards, pill nav, smooth scroll |\n| `references/foundations.md` | Grid, layout patterns, alignment, spacing, visual hierarchy, typography, token sheet |\n| `references/color.md` | Building palettes, 60-30-10, contrast, dark mode, gradients, color psychology by industry |\n| `references/components.md` | Nav, hero, buttons, cards, forms, footer, modals — specs and states |\n| `references/motion.md` | Durations, easing, scroll reveals, page transitions, micro-interactions, reduced motion |\n| `references/3d.md` | When to use 3D, which tool (CSS 3D, Spline, Three.js/R3F, model-viewer), performance budget, fallbacks |\n| `references/styles.md` | 8 style presets with fonts, palettes, layout and motion character |\n| `references/site-types/*.md` | Section order and conversion rules per site type |\n| `references/qa-checklist.md` | The final pass before calling it done |\n| `references/anti-patterns.md` | What makes a site look generic or AI-made, and the fix |\n| `references/sources.md` | Where each rule comes from |\n| `examples/editorial-bold-tokens.css` | A real token sheet from a shipped award-style portfolio |\n</file>\n\n<file path=\"references/art-direction.md\">\n# Art direction: making it memorable, not just correct\n\nRules (grid, contrast, spacing) make a site **correct**. They do not make it **good**. A site built only from rules looks like every other AI/template site. This file is the difference. Do it **before** any layout.\n\n## 1. The concept (one sentence, mandatory)\n\nWrite a one-line concept that could only belong to this brand. It drives the hero, the signature interaction and the copy voice.\n\n| Weak (generic) | Strong (concept) |\n|---|---|\n| \"A warm, modern ceramics store\" | \"Every mug carries its maker's thumbprint — the site is a wall of thumbprints you can shop\" |\n| \"A professional dispatch company site\" | \"A live load board: the hero *is* a ticking map of trucks finding loads right now\" |\n| \"A clean SaaS landing page\" | \"The page is the product: you edit the hero headline with the tool itself\" |\n| \"A personal portfolio\" | \"This portfolio talks back\" (voice AI guide + orb) |\n\nTest: if you swap the logo for a competitor's and the page still works, there is no concept. Start again.\n\n## 2. The signature moment (pick exactly one)\n\nOne thing people will describe to a friend. Spend 30% of the design effort here.\n- An interactive hero object tied to the product (rotate the real product, a talking orb, a live map, a draggable before/after).\n- A typographic stunt (headline that reacts to cursor/scroll, giant type that becomes the navigation).\n- A tactile interaction (stack of cards to flick through, product on a turntable, a \"pour\" or \"fill\" scroll animation).\n- An unusual layout idea (a store laid out like a market stall shelf, a menu that reads like a printed menu, a case study as a magazine spread).\nEverything else on the page stays quiet so this moment lands.\n\n## 3. Imagery decides 50% of the quality\n\n- **Never draw products, people or places with SVG/CSS shapes.** Flat vector \"illustrations\" of real products read as fake instantly.\n- Order of preference: the client's real photos → commissioned/AI-generated photography in one consistent art direction → licensed photography (Unsplash/Pexels) chosen for one consistent look → 3D renders. Placeholder boxes with a clear label beat fake illustrations.\n- One photographic direction for the whole site: same light (e.g. hard window light), same background family, same angle, same color grade. Write it down (\"overhead, hard morning light, linen and concrete, warm grade\").\n- Crop with intent: tight details, hands using the product, extreme close-ups of texture, scale shots. Avoid the centered \"product on blank background\" for hero images.\n- Let images break the grid on purpose: full-bleed, overlapping a section edge, oversized against small type.\n\n## 4. Typography with a point of view\n\n- The safe pairs are now the AI default: serif display + italic accent word + DM Sans/Inter, or Inter everywhere. Avoid unless the concept demands it.\n- Choose a display face that carries the brand: condensed grotesk, wide extended sans, quirky serif, monospace, a variable font you animate. Look beyond Google's top 20 (Fontshare, Pangram Pangram, Velvetyne, Collletttivo free/open fonts).\n- Use **scale contrast**: really big next to really small. Medium-everything is the template look.\n- Let type do layout work: headlines that span the full width, vertical text, numbers as graphic elements.\n\n## 5. Color with a point of view\n\n- Avoid the current AI defaults: cream + terracotta + olive; purple→blue gradient on white; black + neon green \"tech\". Use them only when the brand truly owns them.\n- Derive the palette from the concept or the product (the actual glaze colors, the client's truck livery, the city's light).\n- Be braver with fewer colors: one dominant brand color used at full strength on large areas often looks more designed than a \"balanced\" palette.\n\n## 6. Layout: break the template rhythm\n\nTemplate rhythm: eyebrow → headline → 2 buttons → icon strip → \"heading + 3/4 cards\" × 5 → newsletter → footer. Avoid it.\n- Vary section shapes: one full-bleed image, one giant-type section, one dense grid, one quiet single-column, one horizontal scroll.\n- Make at least one section unexpected for its type (reviews as handwritten notes on product photos; the value props as a single sentence in huge type instead of 4 icons).\n- Asymmetry on a strict grid: offset columns, images that start at column 2 and end at 12.\n- Fewer, bigger things. A home page with 5 strong sections beats 10 polite ones.\n\n## 7. Copy voice\n\n- No filler (\"Elevate your\", \"Discover our\", \"Crafted with care\", \"Seamless\", \"Unlock\"). These are AI tells.\n- Specific, concrete, a little surprising. \"Fired twice, at 1,240°C\" beats \"High-quality ceramics\".\n- Headings that a person would say out loud.\n\n## 8. Reference hunt (do it, don't skip it)\n\nBefore designing, look at 5–10 award-level sites in the same or a neighbouring category (Awwwards, Godly, Siteinspire, Land-book). Note for each: the concept, the signature moment, the type, the image direction. Steal principles, never layouts.\n\n## 9. Variety rule (no house style)\n\nEach new site must not look like the last one you built. Before the brief, list the previous project's choices and change **at least 4 of these 6**:\n1. Base lightness (dark ↔ light ↔ saturated color base)\n2. Display type family (condensed ↔ wide/extended ↔ serif ↔ rounded ↔ mono)\n3. Accent hue\n4. Hero structure (poster stage, full-bleed photo, split, editorial grid, product-first, type-only, interactive canvas)\n5. Signature moment mechanic (counter, cursor trail, drag, stamp/paint, sound, 3D, scroll story, filter/remix)\n6. Which studio-grade patterns you use (pick a different set of 5 from `studio-grade.md`)\n\nAlso vary voice (blunt / poetic / playful / technical) and imagery direction (moody studio / bright street / flat-lay / candid).\n\n## 10. Second pass: \"make it better\" moves\n\nAfter the first build works, do one polish pass with these (each one tied to the concept, not added for its own sake):\n- **Make the signature moment physical:** don't stop at a number or headline — give it light, heat, sound or motion that matches the concept (e.g. a kiln temperature that climbs with an ember glow that cools as you scroll away).\n- **A brand-voice ticker** between hero and content: the brand's real facts as a marquee in the accent color (pause on hover, `aria-hidden`, still on reduced motion).\n- **Cursor-follow previews** for list/table layouts on desktop: hovering a row shows a large photo that trails the cursor (pointer: fine + ≥1024px only; lerp ~0.15).\n- **Fill empty grid space with evidence**, not decoration: a real process/studio photo with a caption beside a statement.\n- **Navigation that survives scrolling:** header turns solid after the hero, hides on scroll down, returns on scroll up.\n- **A designed mobile menu:** full-screen, big display-type links that stagger in, contact shortcut at the bottom, Esc/close returns focus.\n\n## 11. Art direction brief (write before building)\n\n```\nConcept: (one line)\nSignature moment: (one thing)\nImage direction: (light, background, angle, grade, crop)\nType: display = …, body = …, why it fits the concept\nPalette: (2–4 colors), where it comes from\nLayout idea: (what makes this page's structure unusual)\nVoice: (3 adjectives + one sample headline)\nWhat we will NOT do: (3 template moves we avoid)\n```\n</file>\n\n<file path=\"references/anti-patterns.md\">\n# Anti-patterns: what makes a site look generic or \"AI-made\" (and the fix)\n\n## The 2026 AI-default look (check these first)\n\nThese were \"tasteful\" two years ago; now every AI generator produces them, so they read as slop. If the page has **3 or more**, redesign — don't patch.\n\n- [ ] Cream/off-white background + terracotta/rust accent + olive/sage secondary.\n- [ ] Serif display headline (Fraunces, Playfair, Instrument Serif) with **one word in italic and accent color**.\n- [ ] Small uppercase letter-spaced \"eyebrow\" label above every heading (\"NEW · THE X COLLECTION\").\n- [ ] Hero = eyebrow + headline + one-paragraph subline + two pill buttons (filled + outline) + ★★★★★ rating line.\n- [ ] A strip of 3–4 icon + bold title + grey line \"value props\" (Free shipping / Returns / Secure / Quality).\n- [ ] Every section is \"eyebrow + heading on the left, 'View all' link on the right, grid of cards below\".\n- [ ] Products or people drawn as flat SVG/CSS shapes instead of photos.\n- [ ] Arch-shaped image masks + paper grain added as \"texture\" without a reason.\n- [ ] Three testimonial cards in a row with initial-letter avatars.\n- [ ] Newsletter block in the accent color with \"Get 10% off\".\n- [ ] Copy with \"crafted\", \"elevate\", \"everyday\", \"seamless\", \"thoughtfully\", \"small batches\", \"made to be used\".\n- [ ] Dark mode version: near-black + one neon accent + glowing gradient blobs + bento grid of features.\n- [ ] Inter/DM Sans everywhere with a purple→blue gradient.\n\nFix: go back to `art-direction.md` — concept, signature moment, image direction — and rebuild the hero and section shapes from the concept, not from the template.\n\n## General tells\n\n| Tell | Why it looks cheap | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Purple-to-blue gradient on white, Inter everywhere, emoji icons | The default of every template/AI output | Pick a style preset; a real palette from the brand; a display font with character; a consistent icon set (Lucide, Phosphor, custom) |\n| Centered everything | No hierarchy, no reading edge | Left-align content blocks; center only heroes/short intros |\n| Every section is \"heading + 3 cards with icons\" | Monotone rhythm, nothing memorable | Vary layouts: bento, split, big number, quote, full-bleed image, list |\n| Vague headline (\"Empowering your future with innovative solutions\") | Says nothing; fails the 5-second test | Customer outcome in plain words + specifics/numbers |\n| Fake/placeholder content left in (John Doe, 10k+ happy clients) | Destroys trust instantly | Real content or clearly marked placeholders for the client |\n| Too many accent colors / everything bold | Nothing stands out | 60-30-10; one accent per screen |\n| Random spacing (13px here, 22px there) | Feels unfinished even if you can't say why | 8pt scale tokens only |\n| Elements \"almost\" aligned | Subconscious sloppiness | Grid overlay check; shared edges |\n| Generic stock photos (handshakes, people pointing at screens) | Instantly recognized as stock | Real photos, product UI, illustrations or 3D in brand colors |\n| Huge drop shadows, many radii | Dated, inconsistent | 2–3 elevation levels, one radius family, tinted soft shadows |\n| Animations on everything, bouncing on scroll | Distracting, slow, amateur | One hero moment; quiet, short reveals elsewhere; reduced motion |\n| Scroll-jacking / slow smooth scroll on mobile | Users feel out of control | Native scroll on touch; light Lenis on desktop only |\n| Auto-playing carousels in the hero | Low engagement (NN/g); users miss slides | One strong static hero message; carousels only user-controlled |\n| Popups on load, chat bubbles that cover the CTA | Annoying, hurts conversion and SEO | Delay/intent-based, easy to close, place widgets away from CTAs |\n| Placeholder-only form labels, \"Submit\" buttons | Unusable and unclear | Labels above fields; verb+object button text |\n| Low-contrast grey text on white / thin light fonts | Unreadable, fails WCAG | Check contrast; weight ≥ 400 for body |\n| Icons without labels in nav | Mystery meat navigation | Text labels (icons optional) |\n| Inconsistent image ratios in a grid | Messy, cheap | Fixed `aspect-ratio` + `object-fit: cover` |\n| Heavy 3D/video that delays content | Slow LCP, bounce | Poster first, lazy-load, fallback |\n| Same layout on mobile as desktop, just squeezed | Tiny text, cramped tap targets | Re-compose for mobile: stack, reorder, bigger targets, sticky CTA |\n| Hamburger menu on desktop with lots of space | Hides navigation for no reason | Visible nav on desktop |\n| Footer with dead links / \"© 2021\" | Looks abandoned | Real links, current year computed |\n| Lorem ipsum headings in the final build | Unprofessional | Write real copy (benefit-led, specific) |\n\n## Signature-detail ideas (make it feel designed)\nPick 1–3 and use them consistently:\n- Numbered section labels in mono (\"(02) — Services\").\n- A hairline rule system and a consistent kicker line above each section.\n- Paper grain or subtle noise on light sections.\n- One custom illustrated or 3D motif repeated across the page.\n- A distinctive button shape (pill with an arrow that slides, or brutal offset shadow).\n- Oversized numbers for stats or steps.\n- Image masks with a recurring shape (arch, rounded rect, circle).\n- A custom cursor or a micro-interaction that echoes the brand.\n- A marquee of keywords/clients in the brand's voice.\n</file>\n\n<file path=\"references/studio-grade.md\">\n# Studio-grade: from \"good landing page\" to \"experience\"\n\nA page can have a concept, real photos and clean layout and still feel like a template. Award-level sites feel like an **experience**: they have an entrance, a composed stage, a scroll choreography and living details. These patterns come from a shipped award-style portfolio (`examples/editorial-bold-tokens.css`). Use **at least 5 of the 10** on any brand/marketing home page (fewer on checkout or dashboards).\n\nEvery pattern needs: reduced-motion fallback, keyboard access, content visible without JS, and ≤ 60fps cost.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Entrance (intro gate)\nA 1.5–2.5s branded preloader that **tells the concept** (a boot log, a kiln heating up, a counter), then a curtain reveal. Skippable (click / Esc / \"Skip\"), shown once per session, safety timeout so the site never stays hidden.\n\n```css\n.gate{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:999;background:var(--ink);animation:gate-safety .3s 4s forwards}\n@keyframes gate-safety{to{opacity:0;visibility:hidden}}\n.gate.closing{animation:curtain 1.1s cubic-bezier(.77,0,.175,1) forwards}\n@keyframes curtain{to{clip-path:inset(0 0 100% 0)}}\nhtml:not(.entered) .hero-letter>span{translate:0 102%}   /* hero waits for the gate */\n```\n- Count a big number 0→100 (or 20→1,240°C) with mono \"log\" lines appearing (`[0.42] loading batch 046 ✓`).\n- On close: set `html.entered` → the hero entrance starts.\n- `sessionStorage` flag to skip on return visits; reduced motion → skip the gate entirely.\n\n## 2. Full-bleed wordmark with per-letter reveal\nThe brand name as the biggest thing on the page, filling the width exactly.\n```css\n.name{font-size:min(calc((100vw - var(--pad)*2) * .46), calc((100svh - 150px) * .9));line-height:.8;white-space:nowrap;text-align:center}\n.letter{display:inline-block;overflow:clip;padding-top:.12em;margin-top:-.12em}\n.letter>span{display:inline-block;transition:translate 1.3s var(--ease-out) calc(.35s + var(--i)*.07s), transform .6s var(--ease-spring), color .35s}\n@media (hover:hover) and (pointer:fine){.letter:hover>span{color:var(--accent);transform:translateY(-.07em) rotate(-3deg)}}\n```\nTune the multiplier (`.46`) to the word length and font so it spans the container edge-to-edge. Wrap each letter in JS or server-side with `--i`, keep an `aria-label` on the parent and `aria-hidden` on letters.\n\n## 3. Poster-composed hero (a stage, not a column)\nPlace 4–6 elements absolutely around a central object, like a magazine cover:\n- top-left: tagline with an accent arrow · top-right: live status (dot + \"Firing 046 open\") · mid-left: a pill that **bleeds off the left edge** (`left: calc(-1 * var(--pad) - 48px)`) holding location/info with a spinning icon · center: the signature object (canvas, photo cut-out, 3D) · a speech-bubble CTA with a hairline pointing at the object · bottom: the wordmark + a scroll indicator line with a scanning accent.\n- Mobile: same pieces re-stacked; keep the object and wordmark.\n- Elements enter in a stagger after the gate (header .2s, tagline/status .75s, pill .9s, bubble 1.3s).\n\n## 4. A living signature object\nSomething in the hero that is always subtly alive and reacts: a dot-sphere orb, ember particles, a rotating product, a fluid gradient. Canvas 2D is usually enough (see `3d.md`). Give it **states** (idle / hover / active) and let it react to cursor or scroll. Pause off-screen; static frame on reduced motion.\n\n## 5. Sections that slide over each other\nThe next section rises over the previous with rounded top corners and a shadow, so the page feels layered like cards.\n```css\n.over{position:relative;z-index:2;margin-top:calc(-1 * var(--radius));border-radius:var(--radius) var(--radius) 0 0;box-shadow:0 -30px 60px -30px rgb(0 0 0 / .5)}\n```\nAlternate themes (dark → light-with-grain → dark → accent) by redefining tokens per section.\n\n## 6. Sticky stacking cards with scroll-driven depth\nWork/products/features as big cards that stick and stack; the card underneath scales down and darkens.\n```css\n.stack{--stick:96px;--step:22px;view-timeline:--stack block}\n.stack>li{position:sticky;top:calc(var(--stick) + var(--i)*var(--step))}\n@supports (animation-timeline: view()){\n  .stack .card{animation:card-back linear both;animation-timeline:--stack;\n    animation-range:exit-crossing calc(var(--i)/var(--n)*100%) exit-crossing calc((var(--i)+1)/var(--n)*100%)}\n  @keyframes card-back{to{scale:.9}}\n}\n```\nCard = 5/7 split: info (number, category, year, huge title, chips) + big visual. Whole card clickable. Hover: 3D tilt of the visual (`rotateX/Y` from pointer, ±6°).\n\n## 7. Navigation as an object\n- Desktop: the hero has its own inline header; after the hero, a **floating glass pill nav** drops in at top-center (`translate:-50% -140%` → `0`, `.7s ease-out`), dark glass, links + one accent CTA pill.\n- Mobile: a **bottom tab bar** pill (thumb zone) that springs up after the hero, active tab filled with the light color. Respect `env(safe-area-inset-bottom)`.\n\n## 8. Inertial smooth scroll (desktop wheel only)\n```js\nimport Lenis from 'lenis';   // npm i lenis\nif (!matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {\n  const lenis = new Lenis({ lerp: 0.09, smoothWheel: true, anchors: { offset: -16 } });\n  (function raf(t){ lenis.raf(t); requestAnimationFrame(raf); })(performance.now());\n}\n```\nTouch keeps native scrolling. Never hijack scroll direction or speed beyond this.\n\n## 9. Giant section headings with a counter\nSection titles at 10–13vw (`line-height:.82`) with a side column: short description + a big accent count (\"13 projects\", \"06 pieces left\"). Numbered kickers \"(02) — Work\" above in mono.\n\n## 10. Micro-life everywhere (pick 3–4)\n- Scan line in the hero foot (1px rule with a moving accent segment, 2.6s).\n- Spinning globe/icon in the location pill (12s linear).\n- Status dot with a soft pulse ring.\n- Letter hover bounce on display type.\n- Magnetic CTA (≤ 10px pull).\n- Marquee of brand facts.\n- Live data: clock, countdown, \"x people viewing\", local time of the studio.\n- Custom selection color in the accent.\n\n---\n\n## Budget check\n- Intro ≤ 2.5s, skippable. LCP still measured on the real hero text (render it in HTML under the gate).\n- One canvas per view, DPR ≤ 2, paused off-screen.\n- All entrance transforms on `transform/translate/opacity/clip-path` only.\n- Test the whole choreography with reduced motion ON: everything must simply be there, readable, in order.\n</file>\n\n<file path=\"references/site-types/ecommerce.md\">\n# E-commerce / store\n\nGoal: help people **find → trust → buy** with zero friction. Most rules here come from Baymard Institute's large-scale checkout and product-page usability studies.\n\n## Home page — section order\n1. Announcement bar (free shipping threshold / offer) — 1 line, dismissible.\n2. Header: logo, search (prominent, open field on desktop), categories, account, cart with count.\n3. Hero: current campaign or best category, real product/lifestyle photo, one CTA (\"Shop new arrivals\").\n4. Category tiles (4–8) with photos and plain names.\n5. Best sellers / new arrivals product rail.\n6. Value props strip: shipping, returns, payment options (COD where local), support.\n7. Social proof: reviews, UGC photos, press.\n8. Brand story (short) or collection highlight.\n9. Newsletter with a concrete incentive.\n10. Footer with policies, contact, payment icons.\n\n## Product listing (category) page\n- Grid: **2 columns on mobile**, 3–4 on desktop. Same image ratio (4:5 or 1:1), clean background.\n- Card: image (second image on hover), name, price (sale price + struck original), rating + count, color swatches. Quick add on desktop.\n- Filters: sticky sidebar desktop, bottom sheet on mobile with a visible \"Show 124 results\" button. Show applied filters as removable chips. Sort (Featured, Price, Newest, Rating).\n- Show result count. Infinite scroll with \"Load more\" button (keeps footer reachable) or pagination.\n\n## Product page (the money page)\nAbove the fold (mobile too): **images, name, price, rating, variant pickers, Add to cart**.\n- Gallery: 5–8 images incl. scale/in-use/detail shots, zoom, swipe on mobile, video if possible, 3D/AR for physical products where budget allows (see `3d.md`).\n- Variants as visible buttons/swatches (not dropdowns) with out-of-stock states shown, not hidden.\n- Price clarity: total cost hints (shipping, taxes) near the price — unexpected costs are the #1 cart-abandonment reason in Baymard's surveys.\n- Add to cart: big, accent color, **sticky on mobile** when scrolled past. After adding: mini-cart drawer or clear confirmation with \"Checkout\" and \"Continue shopping\".\n- Trust near the button: delivery estimate (\"Arrives by Thu\"), returns policy, secure payment icons, stock (only if true).\n- Description: scannable bullets for key benefits first, then specs table, size guide (with fit info), care, shipping & returns (accordions).\n- Reviews: summary (average, distribution), photos, filter by rating, \"most helpful\". Show negative reviews too — they increase trust.\n- Cross-sell: \"Complete the look\" / \"Frequently bought together\" below.\n\n## Cart\n- Editable quantities, remove, item images, clear subtotal, shipping estimate, discount code field (collapsed link, so it doesn't send people hunting for codes).\n- Free-shipping progress bar if there's a threshold. Prominent \"Checkout\" + express pay (Apple Pay, Google Pay, PayPal, local wallets).\n\n## Checkout (Baymard essentials)\n- **Guest checkout** prominent; account creation optional after purchase (forced accounts are a top abandonment reason).\n- As few fields as possible; single name field or first/last as needed; address autocomplete; ZIP/postcode first to auto-fill city.\n- Steps: Contact → Shipping → Payment → Review (or one-page with sections). Show progress.\n- Order summary always visible (collapsible on mobile). No surprise costs at the last step.\n- Payment: trusted badges, card field formatting with spaces, show card type, CVC help. Local options matter (COD, bank transfer, JazzCash/Easypaisa in Pakistan, UPI in India, etc.).\n- Keep users' data on errors; validate inline; correct keyboard types.\n- Remove header nav distractions during checkout (logo + secure badge + help only).\n\n## Order confirmation\n- Order number, summary, delivery estimate, what happens next, tracking link, account creation offer (prefilled), support contact.\n\n## Search\n- Visible field (not just an icon) on desktop; autocomplete with products, categories and typo tolerance; good \"no results\" page with suggestions.\n\n## Performance targets\n- Product images AVIF/WebP with `srcset`; LCP image = first product photo with `fetchpriority=\"high\"`.\n- Third-party scripts (reviews, chat, pixels) loaded after interaction/idle.\n\n## Structured data / SEO\n- `Product` with `offers`, `aggregateRating`, `review`; `BreadcrumbList`; `Organization`. Unique titles/descriptions per product. Canonical tags on filtered URLs.\n\n## Conversion checklist\n- [ ] Price, variants and Add to cart visible on first mobile screen of the product page.\n- [ ] Sticky Add to cart on mobile.\n- [ ] Shipping cost/time clear before checkout.\n- [ ] Guest checkout available and obvious.\n- [ ] Express payments on cart/checkout.\n- [ ] Reviews with photos on product page.\n- [ ] Returns policy one tap away from the buy button.\n</file>\n\n<file path=\"references/foundations.md\">\n# Foundations: layout, alignment, spacing, hierarchy, typography\n\n## 1. How people actually look at a page (design for this)\n\n- **5-second judgement.** Visitors decide in seconds whether to stay (NN/g: users leave within 10–20s unless the value is clear). The hero must answer *what / for whom / what next*.\n- **Above the fold matters most.** NN/g eye-tracking: ~57% of viewing time is above the fold, ~74% in the first two screenfuls. Put the value proposition and primary CTA there; don't put critical info only at the bottom.\n- **Scanning patterns.**\n  - **F-pattern** on text-heavy pages: users read the first lines, then scan the left edge. → Front-load headings and bullets with keywords; keep a strong left edge.\n  - **Z-pattern** on sparse pages (hero, landing): logo top-left → nav top-right → diagonal → CTA bottom-right of the block.\n  - **Layer-cake** (NN/g): users scan headings only. → Headings must make sense on their own.\n- **Left side gets more attention** (NN/g: ~80% of fixations on the left half in LTR). Put labels, key content and nav on the left; flip for RTL.\n- **Banner blindness:** anything that looks like an ad (boxed, flashy, right column) is ignored. Don't style important content like a banner.\n\n## 2. Psychology laws to apply\n\n| Law | Rule | Apply it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Jakob's law | Users expect your site to work like others they use | Logo top-left links home, cart top-right, nav at top, search with a magnifier icon |\n| Hick's law | More choices → slower decisions | ≤ 7 top nav items, 1 primary CTA, 3 pricing tiers |\n| Fitts's law | Big, close targets are faster | Primary CTA large, near the content it acts on; sticky mobile CTA in thumb zone |\n| Miller / chunking | People hold few items at once | Group into 3–5 items; split long forms into steps |\n| Von Restorff | The different thing is remembered | Only the primary CTA gets the accent color |\n| Gestalt proximity | Close = related | Gap inside a group < gap between groups (e.g. 8–16px vs 48–96px) |\n| Gestalt similarity | Look alike = act alike | All links one style; all cards in a set the same structure |\n| Serial position | First and last items are remembered | Put the strongest benefit first and the CTA last in a list/section |\n| Doherty threshold | Response < 400ms keeps flow | Optimistic UI, skeletons, instant feedback on click |\n| Aesthetic-usability effect | Beautiful feels easier to use | Polish is not optional; it buys trust and patience |\n\n## 3. Grid\n\n- **Columns:** 12 on desktop (≥1024), 8 on tablet (768–1023), 4 on mobile (<768).\n- **Container:** max content width 1200–1440px; body text column max ~680px (≈ 65 characters).\n- **Side padding:** `clamp(20px, 4vw, 56px)`. Gutters: `clamp(16px, 2vw, 28px)`.\n- **Breakpoints:** design at 375, 768, 1024, 1440. Test 320 (small Android) and 1920.\n- Use CSS Grid for page layout, Flexbox for one-direction component layout. Use `subgrid` to align card internals across a row.\n- Container queries (`@container`) for components that live in different widths (cards, sidebars).\n\n```css\n.grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(12, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--gutter); }\n@media (max-width: 1023px) { .grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(8, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }\n@media (max-width: 767px)  { .grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }\n```\n\n## 4. Alignment rules\n\n1. **Pick one alignment edge per section** (usually left). Centered text only for short blocks (≤ 3 lines): heroes, section intros, CTAs.\n2. **Never center long paragraphs or lists.** Ragged left edges slow reading.\n3. **Everything sits on the grid.** Images, text and buttons start on a column line. Check with a grid overlay.\n4. **Align text baselines** across columns (headings in a row of cards line up; use `subgrid` or fixed title heights with `line-clamp`).\n5. **Optical alignment:** icons and round shapes look small and misaligned when mathematically aligned. Nudge play icons right ~1–2px, make circles ~5–10% bigger than squares, hang quote marks outside the text edge.\n6. **Numbers in tables right-aligned** with `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`. Text left-aligned.\n7. **Buttons in a row** share height; icon + label vertically centered; icon gap 8px.\n8. **Form labels** above inputs (fastest to scan, works on mobile), left-aligned.\n\n## 5. Spacing system (8-point)\n\nScale: `4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64, 96, 128, 160`.\n\n| Use | Size |\n|---|---|\n| Icon ↔ label | 8 |\n| Inside a component (padding) | 12–24 |\n| Between related items in a group | 8–16 |\n| Between groups within a section | 32–64 |\n| Section padding (vertical) | `clamp(72px, 10vw, 160px)` |\n| Heading → its body text | 12–24 (closer to its content than to the previous block) |\n\n- **Internal ≤ external:** padding inside a card ≤ the gap between cards' groups.\n- **Whitespace is a luxury signal.** Premium brands use more of it; discount/news sites use less. Match the brand.\n- **Vertical rhythm:** keep line-heights and spacing on multiples of 4.\n\n## 6. Visual hierarchy (what is seen first)\n\nRank every element on the page as 1st, 2nd, 3rd. Then use these levers, strongest first:\n\n1. **Size** (the biggest thing is read first)\n2. **Color/contrast** (the accent pulls the eye; only one thing per screen should be the accent)\n3. **Weight** (bold vs regular)\n4. **Position** (top-left, center of Z)\n5. **Whitespace** around it (isolation = importance)\n6. **Motion** (use once per screen at most)\n\nCheck: blur the screenshot (or squint). You should still see the headline, then the CTA, then supporting content. If everything is equally loud, nothing is.\n\nDe-emphasize, don't just emphasize: make secondary text lighter (60–70% opacity of the text color) instead of making primary text bigger and bigger.\n\n## 7. Layout patterns that work\n\n| Pattern | Best for | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Split hero (text left, visual right) | SaaS, business, apps | Text 5–6 cols, visual 6–7 cols; stack on mobile with text first |\n| Full-bleed hero with big type | Portfolio, brand, fashion | Type as the visual; keep the CTA visible |\n| Bento grid | Features, portfolio, stats | Mixed tile sizes on a strict grid; 1 hero tile; consistent radius and gap |\n| Alternating zig-zag | Feature explainers | Image/text sides alternate; max 3–4 rows before it gets boring |\n| Card grid | Products, blog, projects | 2 cols mobile (products) or 1 col (content), 3–4 desktop; same aspect ratio images |\n| Sticky scroll storytelling | Product reveals, process | Pinned visual, text scrolls past; keep it short (3–5 steps) |\n| Editorial asymmetry | Premium, portfolio, agencies | Deliberate off-center blocks, big numbers, oversized type — but still on the grid |\n| Horizontal marquee | Logos, clients, keywords | Pause on hover, `aria-hidden` duplicates, respect reduced motion |\n| Logo/trust strip | Everything selling | Right under the hero; greyscale logos at the same optical size |\n\n**Section rhythm:** alternate density (dense → airy → dense) and background (base → tinted → base, or dark → light) so the eye knows a new section began. Max 2–3 background colors across the page.\n\n## 8. Typography\n\n### Choosing fonts\n- **Max 2 families** (display + body), optional monospace for labels/data.\n- Pair by **contrast**: expressive display (serif, condensed, or geometric) + calm, readable sans for body.\n- Body fonts need: large x-height, open apertures, good numerals, many weights. Proven: Inter, Satoshi, Geist, General Sans, DM Sans, Manrope, Plus Jakarta Sans, IBM Plex Sans, Source Sans 3.\n- Display fonts give the personality (see `styles.md` for pairs).\n- Use variable fonts and `font-display: swap`; preload only the 1–2 files used above the fold; subset to the languages you need.\n\n### Type scale\nUse a modular scale. Ratio **1.25** (Major Third) for UI-dense sites, **1.333** (Perfect Fourth) for marketing, **1.5+** for editorial/portfolio drama. Make it fluid with `clamp()`:\n\n```css\n--step--1: clamp(0.83rem, 0.8rem + 0.15vw, 0.9rem);   /* small, captions */\n--step-0:  clamp(1rem,    0.95rem + 0.25vw, 1.125rem); /* body 16–18px */\n--step-1:  clamp(1.25rem, 1.15rem + 0.5vw,  1.5rem);   /* h4 */\n--step-2:  clamp(1.56rem, 1.35rem + 1vw,    2rem);     /* h3 */\n--step-3:  clamp(1.95rem, 1.6rem + 1.8vw,   2.8rem);   /* h2 */\n--step-4:  clamp(2.44rem, 1.8rem + 3.2vw,   4.2rem);   /* h1 */\n--step-5:  clamp(3rem,    2rem + 6vw,       8rem);     /* display/hero */\n```\n\n### Text rules\n| Property | Body | Headings | Display (huge) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Size | 16–18px (never < 16 on mobile inputs: iOS zooms) | scale steps | 64–200px |\n| Line-height | 1.5–1.7 | 1.1–1.25 | 0.85–1.0 |\n| Letter-spacing | 0 | −0.01 to −0.02em | −0.02 to −0.04em (tighter as it grows) |\n| Line length | 45–75 characters (`max-width: 65ch`) | ≤ ~20 characters per line in heroes | — |\n\n- ALL CAPS only for short labels/display, with +0.04–0.1em tracking (except condensed display faces).\n- `text-wrap: balance` on headings, `text-wrap: pretty` on paragraphs.\n- Avoid widows in headlines (one word alone on the last line).\n- Paragraph spacing ≈ 1em; no indents on the web.\n- Links in body text: underlined (or clearly distinct) — color alone is not enough (WCAG 1.4.1).\n- Use real typographic characters: ’ “ ” — … ×.\n\n## 9. Images\n\n- One consistent treatment across the site: same aspect ratios per context (products 4:5 or 1:1, blog 16:9 or 3:2, team 4:5), same corner radius, same color grade.\n- Real photos > stock. If stock, pick candid, unposed, consistent lighting.\n- Faces and eyes direct attention: a person looking toward the CTA pulls the eye to it.\n- Always set `width`/`height` or `aspect-ratio` (prevents CLS). Use AVIF/WebP, `srcset`/`sizes`, `loading=\"lazy\"` below the fold, `fetchpriority=\"high\"` on the LCP image only.\n- Text over images needs a scrim (gradient overlay) to keep 4.5:1 contrast.\n\n## 10. Token sheet (write this first, every project)\n\n```css\n:root {\n  /* Color — see color.md */\n  --bg: ; --surface: ; --surface-2: ;\n  --text: ; --text-muted: ; --border: ;\n  --primary: ; --primary-hover: ; --on-primary: ;\n  --accent: ; --success: ; --warning: ; --danger: ;\n\n  /* Type */\n  --font-display: ; --font-body: ; --font-mono: ;\n  /* --step--1 … --step-5 (above) */\n\n  /* Space (8pt) */\n  --space-1: 4px; --space-2: 8px; --space-3: 12px; --space-4: 16px; --space-5: 24px;\n  --space-6: 32px; --space-7: 48px; --space-8: 64px; --space-9: 96px; --space-10: 128px;\n  --section-y: clamp(72px, 10vw, 160px);\n  --pad: clamp(20px, 4vw, 56px);\n  --container: 1280px;\n\n  /* Shape */\n  --radius-sm: 8px; --radius: 16px; --radius-lg: 28px; --radius-pill: 999px;\n  --shadow-1: 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / .06), 0 1px 1px rgb(0 0 0 / .04);\n  --shadow-2: 0 8px 24px -8px rgb(0 0 0 / .18);\n  --shadow-3: 0 24px 60px -20px rgb(0 0 0 / .35);\n\n  /* Motion — see motion.md */\n  --ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);\n  --ease-in-out: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1);\n  --ease-spring: cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1);\n  --dur-1: 120ms; --dur-2: 200ms; --dur-3: 320ms; --dur-4: 600ms;\n}\n```\n\n**Radius rule:** nested corners = outer radius − padding (a card with 24px radius and 8px padding holds an image with 16px radius). Keep one radius family across the site.\n\n**Shadow rule:** one light source (from above). Shadows tinted with the background hue, not pure black, on colored backgrounds. Use 2–3 elevation levels only.\n</file>\n\n<file path=\"references/color.md\">\n# Color\n\n## 1. Build a palette in 5 steps\n\n1. **Pick the brand hue** from the logo/brief or from industry fit (table below).\n2. **Build a neutral ramp** that is slightly tinted toward the brand hue (never pure grey `#808080`; tinted neutrals look designed). 10–12 steps from near-white to near-black.\n3. **Build the brand ramp** (50 → 950). Work in **OKLCH**: keep hue constant, step lightness evenly, reduce chroma at the very light and very dark ends.\n4. **Pick one accent** for the primary CTA and key highlights only. It must be the most saturated/contrasting thing on the page.\n5. **Add semantic colors:** success (green), warning (amber), danger (red), info (blue). Tune them to the palette's lightness so they don't look pasted in.\n\n```css\n/* OKLCH ramp example (brand hue 250 = blue) */\n--brand-50:  oklch(97% 0.02 250);\n--brand-100: oklch(93% 0.04 250);\n--brand-300: oklch(78% 0.10 250);\n--brand-500: oklch(60% 0.16 250);  /* base */\n--brand-700: oklch(45% 0.14 250);\n--brand-900: oklch(28% 0.08 250);\n```\n\n## 2. Distribution: 60-30-10\n\n- **60%** dominant: background/neutral surfaces.\n- **30%** secondary: text, cards, secondary surfaces, one supporting brand color.\n- **10%** accent: CTAs, links, active states, key numbers.\n\nIf the accent is everywhere, it stops pulling attention (Von Restorff). Rule of thumb: **one accent element per screen** gets the eye first.\n\n## 3. Contrast (WCAG 2.2, non-negotiable)\n\n| What | Minimum |\n|---|---|\n| Body text | 4.5 : 1 |\n| Large text (≥ 24px regular or ≥ 18.7px bold) | 3 : 1 |\n| UI components, input borders, icons that carry meaning, focus rings | 3 : 1 against neighbours |\n| Placeholder text | treat as body text (4.5 : 1) or don't rely on it |\n| Disabled controls | exempt, but must still look disabled |\n\n- Never use color as the only signal (errors need an icon + text, links need underline or weight).\n- Muted text: use opacity 60–70% of the text color, then **verify** it still passes 4.5:1.\n- Check with a contrast tool (e.g. WebAIM checker, browser devtools). Test for color-blindness (deuteranopia/protanopia) — don't pair red/green as the only distinction.\n\n## 4. Dark mode / dark sites\n\n- Background: not pure `#000`. Use a deep, slightly tinted dark (e.g. `#0E1117`, `#121212`, navy `#14213D`). Pure black + pure white text causes halation/eye strain.\n- Text: off-white (`#EDEDED`, cream `#F4EDE0`), not `#FFF`, for long reading.\n- Elevation in dark mode = **lighter surfaces**, not bigger shadows (surface +4–8% lightness per level).\n- Desaturate brand colors slightly on dark backgrounds; fully saturated colors vibrate.\n- Support both via tokens: swap only token values under `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` or a `[data-theme]` attribute.\n\n## 5. Section color themes\n\nGive whole sections a theme (base / tinted / inverted / accent) by redefining tokens on the section, so every component inside adapts automatically:\n\n```css\n.theme-light  { --bg: var(--cream); --text: var(--navy); }\n.theme-dark   { --bg: var(--navy);  --text: var(--cream); }\n.theme-accent { --bg: var(--coral); --text: var(--navy); }\nsection { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); }\n```\n\nUse max one accent-background section per page (usually the final CTA).\n\n## 6. Gradients, glass and texture\n\n- **Gradients:** neighbouring hues (analogous) or one hue light→dark. Interpolate in OKLCH (`linear-gradient(in oklch, …)`) to avoid muddy grey middles. Big, soft, blurred gradient \"blobs\" behind content add depth cheaply.\n- **Mesh/aurora backgrounds:** keep them behind a scrim so text contrast holds; animate slowly (20s+ loops) if at all.\n- **Glassmorphism:** `backdrop-filter: blur(16–24px)` + semi-transparent fill (6–15% white on dark, 40–60% on light) + 1px light border + soft shadow. Only over interesting backgrounds; ensure text contrast; provide a solid fallback with `@supports not (backdrop-filter: blur(1px))`.\n- **Grain/noise:** a 3–7% opacity SVG noise overlay makes flat colors feel printed and premium and hides gradient banding.\n- **Soft UI (neumorphism):** only for small, low-stakes surfaces; it fails contrast easily. Always give buttons a real fill and a visible border/shadow difference.\n\n## 7. Color by industry (starting points, not laws)\n\n| Industry | Feels right | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Healthcare, clinics | Blues, teals, soft greens, lots of white | Trust + calm; avoid aggressive red except for alerts |\n| Finance, legal, B2B | Navy, deep green, charcoal + one bright accent | Stability; the accent shows modernity |\n| Food, restaurants | Warm reds, oranges, yellows, cream | Appetite; real food photos carry the color |\n| Luxury, fashion | Black, white, cream, one metallic/muted tone | Restraint is the luxury signal; big whitespace |\n| Kids, education | Bright primaries, rounded shapes | Keep contrast for text anyway |\n| Eco, wellness | Greens, earth tones, sand | Natural textures, soft light |\n| Tech, SaaS, AI | Dark bases, violet/blue/electric accents, or clean white + one bold color | Avoid the default \"purple gradient on white\" cliché unless it is on-brand |\n| Logistics, industrial | Dark grey/black + safety red/orange/yellow | Bold condensed type fits |\n| Creative, portfolio | Anything with a strong point of view | The palette *is* the personality; 2 colors + 1 accent |\n\n## 8. Quick palette recipes\n\n- **Monochrome + accent:** one neutral ramp + one loud accent. Hardest to get wrong; very premium.\n- **Duotone:** two brand colors as alternating section backgrounds + text in the other.\n- **Analogous:** 3 neighbouring hues, one dominant. Calm, harmonious.\n- **Complementary accent:** base hue + its opposite only for the CTA. Maximum CTA pop.\n\n## 9. Checks\n\n- [ ] Every text/background pair passes WCAG contrast.\n- [ ] Only one accent color draws the eye per screen.\n- [ ] Neutrals are tinted toward the brand hue.\n- [ ] Semantic colors exist and are used consistently.\n- [ ] Palette works in grayscale (hierarchy still readable).\n- [ ] Dark sections don't use pure black or pure white.\n</file>\n\n<file path=\"references/components.md\">\n# Components: specs and states\n\nEvery interactive component needs: **default, hover, focus-visible, active/pressed, disabled, loading** — and where relevant **empty, error, success**.\n\n## Navigation\n- Logo top-left (links home). Primary links ≤ 7. CTA button far right, visually distinct.\n- Height 64–80px desktop, 56–64px mobile. Sticky header: shrink or gain a background after scrolling ~40px; hide on scroll-down / show on scroll-up for long pages.\n- Current page clearly marked (weight, underline, or pill) + `aria-current=\"page\"`.\n- Mobile: hamburger top-right (or bottom tab bar for app-like sites with 3–5 destinations). Menu opens full-screen or as a sheet, focus is trapped inside, Esc closes, body scroll locked.\n- Labels are plain words (\"Pricing\", \"Work\"), not clever ones. NN/g: vague labels kill findability.\n- Mega menus: organized columns with headings, open on click (or hover with a ~300ms intent delay), never on accidental hover.\n\n## Hero\n- Content: **headline (value, ≤ ~10 words) + subline (who/how, 1–2 lines) + primary CTA + optional secondary CTA + proof (logos, rating, number)**.\n- Headline says the outcome for the customer, not the company's self-description (\"Get high-paying loads every week\" > \"Leading dispatch solutions\").\n- Mobile: headline, CTA and some visual fit in the first screen (use `100svh` carefully; leave a hint of the next section to invite scroll).\n- Visual shows the product/result: product shot, UI screenshot, real photo, or 3D object. Abstract blobs alone don't sell.\n\n## Buttons\n- Height 44–56px (min 44px tap target), horizontal padding 20–32px, label 15–17px, weight 500–700.\n- **Primary:** filled with the accent, one per view. **Secondary:** outline or tonal. **Tertiary:** text link with an arrow.\n- Labels are verbs + object: \"Get a quote\", \"Start free trial\", \"Add to cart\". Not \"Submit\"/\"Click here\".\n- Icon + label gap 8px; trailing arrow nudges 2–4px right on hover.\n- Loading: spinner replaces/sits beside the label; width stays the same; button disabled to stop double submit.\n- Don't disable a submit button to signal invalid forms — let users click and show what's wrong.\n\n## Cards\n- One click target per card (whole card clickable via a stretched link), clear hierarchy: media → eyebrow → title → meta → action.\n- Same image aspect ratio and title line-clamp across a set. Padding 16–24px.\n- Hover: lift 2–4px + shadow or border brighten + image scale 1.03–1.05 inside a clipped frame.\n\n## Forms (highest-impact component for conversion)\n- **Fewer fields = more conversions.** Ask only what you need now (Baymard, NN/g). Mark optional fields, not required ones (or mark both clearly).\n- Single column. Labels above fields (never placeholder-only labels).\n- Correct input types and autocomplete: `type=\"email\" autocomplete=\"email\"`, `type=\"tel\" autocomplete=\"tel\"`, `inputmode=\"numeric\"`, `autocomplete=\"street-address\"` etc.\n- Input height 44–56px, font-size ≥ 16px (prevents iOS zoom), visible 1px+ border with ≥ 3:1 contrast.\n- **Inline validation on blur** (not on every keystroke), success ticks for tricky fields; error text under the field in plain language that says how to fix it (\"Enter a 10-digit phone number\"), with an icon + `aria-describedby`.\n- On submit error: focus the first invalid field and show a summary at the top for long forms.\n- Group long forms into steps with a progress indicator (3–5 steps).\n- After submit: clear success state that says what happens next and when (\"We'll call you within 2 hours\").\n\n## Social proof & trust\n- Testimonials with **real name, photo, role/company** (or city), specific results. Star ratings with count (\"4.8 · 1,240 reviews\").\n- Client logos greyscale, equal optical size, right under the hero.\n- Numbers that matter (\"2,000+ trucks dispatched\"), guarantees, certifications, security badges near payment/CTA.\n- Place proof **next to the decision point** (near the CTA, pricing and checkout), not only on a testimonials page.\n\n## Pricing\n- 3 tiers (Hick's law); highlight the recommended one (badge + accent border + slightly elevated).\n- Monthly/annual toggle showing savings. Feature lists aligned row-by-row for comparison. Price large, period small. Each tier has its own CTA. FAQ right below pricing (objection handling).\n\n## FAQ\n- Accordion with `<details>/<summary>` or proper `aria-expanded` buttons. 5–10 real objections (price, time, refunds, how it works). Add FAQ structured data.\n\n## Modals & drawers\n- Only for focused tasks (quick view, confirm, short forms). Not for content users want to compare or link to.\n- Focus moves in and is trapped; Esc and backdrop click close; focus returns to the trigger. Use `<dialog>`.\n- Never open a popup on page load. Exit-intent or time-based offers: once per session, easy to dismiss, never on mobile full-screen interstitials (Google penalizes intrusive interstitials).\n\n## Footer\n- Repeat key links, contact info (phone, email, address — clickable), social, legal (privacy, terms), newsletter. Secondary sitemap for big sites. A final CTA band above the footer.\n\n## Chat / AI assistant widget\n- Bottom-right floating button, 56–64px, doesn't cover the mobile CTA. Greeting that offers 2–3 starter questions. Typing indicator, streaming replies, human handoff (WhatsApp/phone), lead capture inside the chat once intent is clear.\n\n## Toasts & feedback\n- Bottom (mobile) or top-right/bottom-right (desktop); 4–6s; pausable; `role=\"status\"` (or `alert` for errors). Undo for destructive actions instead of \"Are you sure?\" where possible.\n\n## Empty, loading and error states\n- **Empty:** explain + give the next action (\"No orders yet — Browse products\").\n- **Loading:** skeletons shaped like the content for > 300ms waits; spinners only for short actions.\n- **Error:** human language, what happened, what to do, a retry button. Custom 404 with search and popular links.\n\n## Accessibility baseline for all components\n- Real elements: `<button>` for actions, `<a href>` for navigation.\n- Visible focus, logical tab order, `aria-*` only when native semantics aren't enough.\n- Icons-only buttons have `aria-label`. Decorative images `alt=\"\"`.\n- Hit areas ≥ 44 × 44 px (WCAG 2.2 minimum is 24 × 24; 44 is the Apple/industry comfortable size).\n</file>\n\n<file path=\"references/motion.md\">\n# Motion and animation\n\nMotion must **explain** (where something came from, what changed, what to do next) or **delight once**. If it does neither, remove it.\n\n## 1. Timing\n\n| What | Duration |\n|---|---|\n| Hover/press color, opacity | 100–150ms |\n| Small UI (toggles, checkbox, tooltip) | 150–200ms |\n| Medium (dropdown, card expand, toast) | 200–300ms |\n| Large (modal, drawer, page section) | 300–450ms |\n| Page transitions | 300–600ms |\n| Hero/intro reveal sequences | 600–1200ms total, each item 400–800ms |\n| Ambient loops (gradients, marquees, floating) | 8–40s per cycle, slow |\n\n- NN/g: UI animations feel right at roughly 100–500ms; under 100ms isn't seen, over ~500ms feels sluggish for routine UI.\n- **Exits are faster than entrances** (~70% of the enter duration).\n- Mobile durations ~20–30% shorter than desktop (smaller distances).\n- Response to a click must start within 100ms (feels instant).\n\n## 2. Easing\n\n```css\n--ease-out:    cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);     /* entering, revealing — the default */\n--ease-in:     cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0);     /* leaving the screen */\n--ease-in-out: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1);    /* moving between two on-screen positions */\n--ease-spring: cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1); /* playful overshoot: buttons, badges, toggles */\n```\n\n- Never `linear` for UI movement (only for spinners, marquees, progress).\n- Real things accelerate and decelerate. Springs (Framer Motion / Motion `type: \"spring\"`, stiffness ~300–500, damping ~25–35) feel most natural for drag, layout and gesture.\n\n## 3. Choreography\n\n- **Stagger** lists/grids by 40–80ms per item, cap total stagger at ~400ms (don't make the 12th card wait a second).\n- **Order:** heading → body → visual → CTA. The CTA lands last and steady.\n- **One hero moment per page.** Other sections get quieter reveals.\n- Move things **short distances**: reveals translate 16–40px, not 200px.\n- Animate only `transform` and `opacity` (and `filter`/`clip-path` sparingly). Never animate `width`, `height`, `top`, `left`, `margin` — they cause layout and jank.\n\n## 4. Patterns that look premium\n\n| Pattern | How | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fade-up reveal on scroll | opacity 0→1, translateY 24px→0, `--ease-out`, 600ms | Trigger once at ~15% visibility (IntersectionObserver or CSS `animation-timeline: view()`) |\n| Split-text headline reveal | Lines/words slide up from a mask (`overflow:hidden` per line), stagger 60ms | Keep the real text in the DOM for SEO/screen readers |\n| Image clip reveal | `clip-path: inset(100% 0 0 0)` → `inset(0)` + slight scale 1.1→1 | Great for editorial/portfolio |\n| Parallax | Background moves 10–30% slower than scroll | Subtle only; disable on reduced motion and low-end mobile |\n| Sticky scroll story | Pin a visual, swap states as text scrolls | CSS `position: sticky` + scroll-driven animations or GSAP ScrollTrigger |\n| Magnetic button | Button drifts ≤ 8–12px toward the cursor | Desktop/pointer: fine only |\n| Hover lift | translateY(−2 to −4px) + shadow grows | Cards and buttons |\n| Marquee | Infinite translateX loop, duplicated content `aria-hidden` | Pause on hover/focus; stop on reduced motion |\n| Number count-up | Animate stats when they enter view, 1–1.5s, ease-out | Render the final number in HTML first |\n| Page/view transitions | View Transitions API (`document.startViewTransition`, cross-document `@view-transition { navigation: auto; }`) | Shared-element morph for card → detail |\n| Smooth scroll | Lenis (lerp ~0.1) | Never hijack native scroll on touch; keep anchor links and keyboard working |\n| Cursor follower | Small dot/ring following pointer with lag | Portfolio/creative only; hide on touch; never hide the real cursor on forms |\n\n## 5. Micro-interactions (every interactive element)\n\n- **Hover:** color/elevation change in 150ms.\n- **Press/active:** scale 0.97–0.98.\n- **Focus-visible:** clear 2px ring with offset, ≥ 3:1 contrast. Never `outline: none` without a replacement.\n- **Loading:** button shows spinner + keeps width; skeletons for content (shimmer 1.5s).\n- **Success:** checkmark draw, toast slides in, then auto-dismisses (5s; pause on hover).\n- **Error:** gentle shake (≤ 3 cycles, 300ms) + red message text — never shake alone.\n- **Add to cart:** item flies or badge count bumps with a spring.\n\n## 6. Tools\n\n| Need | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Simple reveals, hovers | CSS transitions/keyframes + IntersectionObserver |\n| Scroll-linked without JS | CSS scroll-driven animations (`animation-timeline: scroll()/view()`) with a fallback where unsupported |\n| React components, layout, gestures, exit animations | Motion (Framer Motion) |\n| Complex timelines, pinning, scroll stories | GSAP + ScrollTrigger |\n| Smooth scrolling | Lenis |\n| Page transitions | View Transitions API |\n| Vector/illustration animation | Lottie (dotLottie) or Rive (interactive, smaller) |\n\n## 7. Accessibility and performance (required)\n\n```css\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  *, *::before, *::after {\n    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;\n    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;\n    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;\n    scroll-behavior: auto !important;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- With reduced motion: no parallax, no auto-playing loops, no scroll hijack; content appears instantly (fades ≤ 150ms are OK).\n- Nothing flashes more than 3 times per second (WCAG 2.3.1).\n- Anything that moves for > 5s automatically needs a pause control (WCAG 2.2.2), e.g. carousels, marquees, background video.\n- Content must be **visible without JS**: start hidden only via a class JS adds (`.js .reveal { opacity: 0 }`).\n- Use `will-change` only during the animation, not permanently.\n- Pause off-screen loops (IntersectionObserver) and when the tab is hidden (`visibilitychange`).\n- Test on a mid-range Android phone: 60fps or cut it.\n</file>\n\n<file path=\"references/styles.md\">\n# Style presets\n\nPick one per site from the client's 3 feeling-words and audience. A preset sets the *character*; tokens (`foundations.md`) and color rules (`color.md`) still apply. Customize at least the palette and one signature detail so no two sites look the same.\n\nEach preset: **feel · fits · fonts · palette · layout · motion · signature details**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Editorial Bold\n- **Feel:** confident, magazine, loud type, crafted.\n- **Fits:** portfolios, agencies, creative studios, personal brands, launches.\n- **Fonts:** condensed display in caps (Tanker, Bebas Neue, Anton, Druk-style) or a high-contrast serif (Fraunces, PP Editorial) + clean sans body (Satoshi, Inter) + mono labels (Geist Mono, JetBrains Mono).\n- **Palette:** deep base (navy/ink) + warm paper (cream) + one hot accent (coral/orange/acid). Sections alternate dark/light; one accent-color section.\n- **Layout:** giant headlines (8–20vw), numbered sections \"(01) — Work\", hairline rules, asymmetric but grid-locked blocks, big numbers, marquees.\n- **Motion:** split-line text reveals, clip-path image reveals, smooth scroll, one interactive hero object.\n- **Signature:** paper grain on light sections, mono micro-labels, sticky kicker lines, custom cursor/orb.\n- **Reference implementation:** `examples/editorial-bold-tokens.css`.\n\n## 2. Clean Minimal (Swiss)\n- **Feel:** calm, precise, trustworthy, modern.\n- **Fits:** SaaS, B2B, consultancies, architecture, fintech.\n- **Fonts:** one neo-grotesk family in several weights (Inter, Geist, Neue Montreal, General Sans).\n- **Palette:** white/near-white + near-black text + one brand color. Neutrals tinted.\n- **Layout:** strict 12-col grid, lots of whitespace, left-aligned, small radius (6–12px), thin borders instead of shadows.\n- **Motion:** minimal — subtle fades, precise hovers, smooth layout transitions.\n- **Signature:** perfect alignment, tabular numbers, crisp product screenshots in device frames.\n\n## 3. Dark Tech / Glow\n- **Feel:** futuristic, powerful, AI, developer.\n- **Fits:** AI products, dev tools, crypto-free tech, gaming tech, cybersecurity.\n- **Fonts:** geometric/grotesk sans (Geist, Space Grotesk, Satoshi) + mono for code/labels.\n- **Palette:** near-black tinted base (#0A0A0F), surfaces +4–8% lightness, electric accent (violet, cyan, lime), glow gradients.\n- **Layout:** bento grids, product UI as hero, code snippets, grid/dot background patterns fading to edges.\n- **Motion:** glowing borders that follow the cursor, beam/line animations, particle or 3D hero, number tickers.\n- **Signature:** radial spotlight on hover, gradient borders, keyboard-shortcut chips.\n\n## 4. Luxury Editorial\n- **Feel:** expensive, quiet, timeless.\n- **Fits:** fashion, jewelry, hotels, real estate, high-end beauty, fine dining.\n- **Fonts:** high-contrast serif display (Cormorant, Playfair Display, Canela-style) + light/refined sans (Montserrat light, Jost, Neue Haas). Wide-tracked caps for labels.\n- **Palette:** black, white, ivory, taupe + one metallic or muted tone (gold, bronze, sage). Very low saturation.\n- **Layout:** full-bleed photography, huge whitespace, small text, centered compositions, slow pacing.\n- **Motion:** slow (600–1200ms) fades and image zooms, elegant page transitions. No bounce.\n- **Signature:** thin lines, small caps, photography doing all the talking.\n\n## 5. Soft UI / Friendly\n- **Feel:** gentle, safe, approachable, human.\n- **Fits:** healthcare, wellness, education, fintech for consumers, mobile apps, family brands.\n- **Fonts:** rounded or humanist sans (Nunito, Plus Jakarta Sans, DM Sans, Manrope).\n- **Palette:** soft pastels or calm blues/teals on light grey-blue backgrounds, one confident primary for CTAs.\n- **Layout:** large radius (16–32px), soft layered shadows, roomy cards, big touch targets, illustrations or soft 3D icons.\n- **Motion:** springy, playful micro-interactions, gentle floating icons.\n- **Signature:** pill buttons, soft 3D/clay icons, friendly illustrations. Watch contrast — soft palettes fail WCAG easily.\n\n## 6. Bold Brutalist / Neo-brutal\n- **Feel:** raw, honest, energetic, young.\n- **Fits:** startups targeting creators, events, music, streetwear, indie products.\n- **Fonts:** heavy grotesk or mono (Archivo Black, Space Mono, Clash Display).\n- **Palette:** saturated flat colors (yellow, pink, blue, lime) + black outlines; no gradients.\n- **Layout:** thick 2–3px black borders, hard offset shadows (`4px 4px 0 #000`), visible grid, stickers, tilted elements.\n- **Motion:** snappy (100–200ms), buttons that press into their shadow.\n- **Signature:** offset shadows, marquee tickers, chunky UI. Keep it usable — still on a grid, still accessible.\n\n## 7. Warm Organic\n- **Feel:** natural, crafted, local, honest.\n- **Fits:** restaurants, cafés, bakeries, eco brands, wellness, handmade goods, local services.\n- **Fonts:** characterful serif (Fraunces, Recoleta, DM Serif) + warm sans (DM Sans, Outfit). Optional handwritten accent used once.\n- **Palette:** earth tones — terracotta, olive, sand, cream, deep brown.\n- **Layout:** organic shapes (blobs, arches), textured backgrounds (paper, grain), overlapping photos, hand-drawn underlines.\n- **Motion:** soft, slow, slight rotation/float on stickers and badges.\n- **Signature:** arch-shaped image masks, stamps/badges, menu-like typography.\n\n## 8. Commerce Clean (conversion-first)\n- **Feel:** clear, fast, trustworthy, shoppable.\n- **Fits:** e-commerce of any size, marketplaces, D2C brands.\n- **Fonts:** one highly legible sans (Inter, Manrope, Satoshi) + optional display for headings.\n- **Palette:** white/light neutral base so products carry the color; one strong CTA color (high contrast); sale/urgency color used sparingly.\n- **Layout:** product-first grids, consistent image ratios, sticky add-to-cart, filters, reviews visible early.\n- **Motion:** fast and functional (add-to-cart feedback, image swaps on hover, quick view).\n- **Signature:** big clean product photography, trust badges near the CTA, clear prices.\n\n---\n\n## Matching guide\n\n| Client says | Try |\n|---|---|\n| \"premium, bold, creative\" | Editorial Bold, Luxury Editorial |\n| \"modern, clean, professional\" | Clean Minimal |\n| \"futuristic, AI, powerful\" | Dark Tech |\n| \"friendly, caring, simple\" | Soft UI |\n| \"fun, young, loud\" | Neo-brutal |\n| \"natural, cozy, local\" | Warm Organic |\n| \"sell more, easy to shop\" | Commerce Clean (+ a brand preset for the home page) |\n\nMixing: one preset leads; borrow at most one signature detail from another (e.g. Commerce Clean + Editorial Bold headlines on the home page).\n</file>\n\n<file path=\"references/qa-checklist.md\">\n# QA checklist (run on screenshots at 375, 768, 1440 + a real phone if possible)\n\nMark each item pass/fail. Fix every fail, re-screenshot, re-check.\n\n## Clarity (5-second test)\n- [ ] Mobile first screen shows: what it is, for whom, primary CTA.\n- [ ] Headline is about the customer's outcome, ≤ ~10 words.\n- [ ] Exactly one primary CTA per screen; it's the most visible element after the headline.\n- [ ] Headings alone tell the story (layer-cake scan).\n- [ ] No lorem ipsum, no invented facts; placeholders are clearly marked.\n\n## Layout & alignment\n- [ ] Everything aligns to the grid and shares left edges within a section.\n- [ ] No horizontal scroll at 320px and 375px.\n- [ ] Long text is left-aligned; centered text only in short blocks.\n- [ ] Card titles/prices/buttons line up across a row.\n- [ ] Spacing uses the 8pt scale; gaps between groups > gaps within groups.\n- [ ] Sections have clear separation (spacing/background/rule).\n- [ ] Nothing important touches the screen edge on mobile (≥ 16–20px gutter).\n- [ ] Images keep consistent aspect ratios and radii per context.\n\n## Typography\n- [ ] ≤ 2 font families (+ mono).\n- [ ] Body ≥ 16px; inputs ≥ 16px.\n- [ ] Line length 45–75 characters on desktop.\n- [ ] Headlines balanced, no orphan word on the last line.\n- [ ] Line-heights: body 1.5–1.7, headings 1.1–1.25.\n- [ ] Fonts load without visible layout shift (fallback metrics or `size-adjust`).\n\n## Color & contrast\n- [ ] All text ≥ 4.5:1 (large ≥ 3:1), UI/focus ≥ 3:1 — checked with a tool.\n- [ ] Accent used only for primary actions/highlights.\n- [ ] Information never conveyed by color alone.\n- [ ] Text over images has a scrim.\n- [ ] Dark mode (if any) checked separately.\n\n## Interaction\n- [ ] Every interactive element has hover, focus-visible, active, disabled, loading states.\n- [ ] Tap targets ≥ 44 × 44px with spacing.\n- [ ] Keyboard: tab through the whole page in a logical order; nothing traps focus except open modals; Esc closes overlays.\n- [ ] Forms: labels, correct input types/autocomplete, inline errors with fix instructions, success state.\n- [ ] Links look like links; buttons look like buttons.\n- [ ] Empty, error and 404 states designed.\n- [ ] Navigation and cart are reachable after scrolling past the hero (sticky/return-on-scroll-up header).\n- [ ] Mobile menu actually opens, traps focus, closes with Esc/close button/link tap, and returns focus to the trigger.\n\n## Motion & 3D\n- [ ] Animations use transform/opacity only, 60fps on desktop, smooth on mid-range mobile.\n- [ ] `prefers-reduced-motion` turns off parallax, loops, scroll effects.\n- [ ] Moving content > 5s has pause; nothing flashes > 3×/s.\n- [ ] 3D has poster + fallback; doesn't delay the headline; pauses off-screen; doesn't trap touch scroll.\n- [ ] Content is visible if JS fails.\n\n## Performance (Lighthouse / PageSpeed Insights, mobile)\n- [ ] LCP ≤ 2.5s, INP ≤ 200ms, CLS ≤ 0.1.\n- [ ] LCP image preloaded/`fetchpriority=\"high\"`, others lazy-loaded.\n- [ ] Images AVIF/WebP, sized with `srcset`, dimensions set.\n- [ ] Fonts: ≤ 2–4 files, preloaded, `font-display: swap`, subset.\n- [ ] Third-party scripts (chat, analytics, pixels) deferred.\n- [ ] JS bundle for the first view kept small; heavy libs (3D, animation) code-split.\n\n## Accessibility\n- [ ] One `h1`; heading levels in order; landmarks (`header`, `nav`, `main`, `footer`).\n- [ ] Alt text on meaningful images, `alt=\"\"` on decorative.\n- [ ] Skip link to main content.\n- [ ] `lang` attribute set; RTL handled if needed.\n- [ ] Zoom to 200% without loss of content; text resizes.\n- [ ] Screen reader smoke test (VoiceOver/NVDA/TalkBack) on nav, hero and main form.\n\n## SEO & sharing\n- [ ] Unique `<title>` (≤ 60 chars) and meta description (≤ 155) per page.\n- [ ] Open Graph + Twitter image (1200 × 630) per key page.\n- [ ] Structured data for the site type (Organization/LocalBusiness/Product/Article/FAQ).\n- [ ] `robots.txt`, `sitemap.xml`, canonical URLs, favicon + app icons.\n- [ ] Clean, descriptive URLs; internal links between related pages.\n\n## Trust & conversion\n- [ ] Social proof near the first CTA and near decision points.\n- [ ] Contact options reachable from every page (sticky on mobile where relevant).\n- [ ] Prices/costs clear; no surprises.\n- [ ] Privacy policy, terms, cookie consent (where legally required) present.\n- [ ] Analytics/conversion events fire on the real success action.\n\n## Cross-browser/device\n- [ ] Chrome, Safari (iOS!), Firefox, Edge.\n- [ ] iOS Safari: `100svh/dvh` used instead of `100vh` for full-height sections; no input zoom; safe-area insets respected for fixed bars.\n- [ ] Landscape phone and 1920+ desktop don't break.\n</file>";
